<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amhrán na bhFiann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">composed late <a href="/years/1909">1909</a> or early <a href="/years/1910">1910</a>; words published <a href="/years/1912">1912</a>; Irish translation printed <a href="/years/1923">1923</a>; adopted as the national anthem 12 July <a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The song was first written in English as &quot;A Soldier's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;, &quot;early in 1910 or late in 1909&quot;, with words by Peadar Kearney and music by his childhood friend and neighbour Patrick Heeney, who composed it on his melodeon with Kearney's help on the refrain; Seán Rogan, later of the Irish Citizen Army, may also have helped and first wrote it down in notation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amhran-na-bhfiann.jpg" alt="Amhrán na bhFiann" width="330" height="589" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peadar Kearney · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irish_national_anthem_(1916).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
